# The pokeemerald engine clone and all hack forks are Nintendo-derived and are
# deliberately kept OUT of this repo's git (see docs/research/34 §7).
# This repo tracks only our ORIGINAL tooling under tools/.
engine/
hacks/
*.gba
*.sym
*.map

# DECIDED 2026-07-26 (the question pokemon/CLAUDE.md had left open for the user).
#
# THE STATES THEMSELVES STAY OUT. An mGBA save state is a verbatim RAM snapshot
# of a running Nintendo-derived ROM — decompressed graphics, text and all — so it
# is squarely the class of thing the block above exists to exclude, and git
# history is permanent. Two further reasons that settle it independently of the
# copyright one:
#   * they are BUILD-SPECIFIC. verify_hack.py hard-fails exit 2 on a ROM-sha
#     mismatch (docs 55 §9.3), and every slice rebuilds the ROM — so a committed
#     state is dead weight by the next slice. Tracking them means a new ~40 KB
#     binary in history per slice, permanently, for artefacts with a one-slice
#     shelf life. That is the worst possible thing to put in git.
#   * tools/make_savestate.py regenerates them from a scripted input
#     timeline, so the RECIPE is tracked even when the output is not.
*.ss1

# THE SIDECARS ARE TRACKED, and deliberately so — this is the half the old
# blanket `saves/` rule got wrong. A .ss1.json is 100% OUR data: rom_sha256,
# savestate_sha256, frame count, final callback, map/camera/player, and the exact
# party values a spec was written against. No Nintendo bytes. It is precisely the
# provenance that tells a future session whether a regenerated state matches the
# one the specs were authored against, and what to re-derive if it does not.
# Ignoring it threw away the only durable record of what a state contained.
#
# CAVEAT that makes the sidecars load-bearing rather than nice-to-have:
# regenerating a state yields a DIFFERENT starter. The boot RNG is seeded from
# the RTC — SeedRngWithRtc(), src/main.c:108, and mGBA derives the RTC from host
# wall-clock time — so the seed is literally the moment you ran the tool. Until
# that is pinned, the sidecar is the only thing that says what the old state held.

# CAPTURED AUDIO IS A NINTENDO ASSET (2026-08-05, doc 76).
# The mAVStream tap records the emulator's actual output, so a .wav from a
# vanilla-music run is Game Freak's composition verbatim -- not a screenshot of
# our own fixture, which is what docs/assets/*.png are. Same three-part argument
# that keeps *.ss1 out: it is Nintendo-derived, it is regenerable by one command
# (verify_hack.py <spec> with "audio": true), and the recipe is tracked while
# the artifact is not. Listen locally; never commit.
# (An earlier form of this rule pointed at a directory the assets had moved out
# of and silently matched nothing -- probe an ignore rule with `git check-ignore`
# whenever it or its subject changes address.)
docs/wiki/assets/*-audio/*.wav
*.pcm
*.pcm.rate

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INHERITED FROM THE PARENT REPO'S ROOT .gitignore, 2026-08-06 (doc 82 F9).
#
# These rules used to live in retro-game-toolkit/.gitignore, one level ABOVE
# pokemon/. `git subtree split` carries the subdirectory's own ignore file and
# knows nothing about its parent's, so extraction silently DROPPED every rule
# here -- and the first verification run in the new repo staged a
# `pokeemerald.sav`: player data out of a Nintendo-derived ROM, exactly the
# class this project fences out of git, caught only by a dry run.
#
# A protection that lives in a file you are not moving is a protection you lose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
build/
*.elf
*.sav
.pipeline/
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
